3.If you nest a list, where does the nested <ul> or <ol> go?
A)Between two <li> tags
B)After the closing </ul> tag
C)Inside an <li> tag  //answer
D)Before the opening <ul> tag

✅ Correct Answer: C. Inside an <li> tag

Explanation (Simple)

When you create a nested list, the new <ul> or <ol> must be placed inside a list item (<li>).

Example:
<ul>
  <li>Fruits
    <ul>
      <li>Apple</li>
      <li>Banana</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li>Vegetables</li>
</ul>

Here, the nested <ul> is inside the <li> containing "Fruits".

Why not the others?
❌ A. Between two <li> tags → Invalid HTML.
❌ B. After the closing </ul> tag → Outside the list.
❌ D. Before the opening <ul> tag → Incorrect placement.

✅ Correct Answer: C. Inside an <li> tag

